corfu is one of greece's most  popular islands for holiday makers with  two and a half  million visitors here in 2019 the talk  was about  over tourism no longer last year's  tourism revenue fell by three quarters  because of covet 19.  and because tourism is the main industry  eight in ten people were left unemployed  and dependent on state aid  many businesses have gone bankrupt eleni  ricikopoulou  she feels lucky to be able to reopen  i worry about going out of business i'm  not optimistic this year  i can't support the shop the expenses  are really high  i'm holding on by my fingernails now  that we've lost a whole season  industry leaders here say they'll be  lucky to reach just 45 percent  of their 2019 income this year  it's a different story on baksi just  south of corfu  the islanders managed to hold on to half  their business during the worst of the  pandemic last year  and believe they'll do even better now  that's because baksi  was developed as a luxury destination in  the 1970s  when europeans started to build fillers  here and then rented them out  to other europeans to offset the cost  pak is a small island without hotels  there are villas and rented apartments  tourists trust because they don't come  into contact with broadly used spaces  the villas are clean and owners try to  keep them disinfected  stefanos fujaros who rents apartments  says half his business is repeat tourism  a level of loyalty hard to find on corfu  there are two and a half thousand  residents on baxi and authorities here  say they have so far vaccinated about  two-thirds of the population  compared to the national average of  under 30 percent  so this island is selling itself as a  covered safe destination  something larger tourism hotspots cannot  do  back sea is certainly doing well as a  luxury destination  the four-month season keeps people in  money the rest of the year  such profits haven't gone unnoticed on  corfu  where a dozen five-star hotels have gone  up in the last fifteen years  coffeats are now seeking to cash in on  those more expensive markets  we're at a turning point we depend on  the british market for 40 percent of our  tourism  we're trying to attract new markets like  china the united arab emirates  kazakhstan and azerbaijan we believe  this is the future  the trend towards luxury locals hope  will ensure the sun never sets  on greek tourism john cerropolos al  jazeera on kofu  and baksi
 

